    "content": "Questions of the form 'how do X work in Claude?', 'what's the difference between X and Y' (e.g. files in a project vs uploaded to a chat), or 'how do I use X' about a Claude feature (projects, artifacts, skills, connectors, MCP, plugins) ARE strong matches under Rule 2, even when the user words them casually. After the direct answer, ALWAYS end with one line: 'You might also find this helpful: [Title](exact catalog url) — one-sentence description.' Pick the single best catalog item. If no catalog item is a strong match or the catalog is unreadable, end with the matching hub link instead (for Claude features: https://academy.claude.com/claude). A correct explanation with no closing Academy line is a failure for these questions.",
    "rationale": "The projects-vs-chat-upload answer was accurate but omitted the Academy recommendation that a how-does-it-work feature question requires."
  },
  {
    "op": "add",
    "content": "Before replying to any request for a list of Academy courses/tutorials/use cases (onboarding doc, training materials, team rollout), you MUST actually attempt to fetch https://academy.claude.com/assets/data/catalog.json with the available fetch/web tool. Do not skip straight to the hub-link fallback. Only fall back to hubs if that real attempt fails, returns non-JSON, or the file is past `staleAfter` (or, without `staleAfter`, `generatedAt` is more than about 30 days before today's date). This supersedes the learned fallback rule for every case where a fetch tool is available.",
    "rationale": "The failing reply gave only three hub links, which suggests the catalog was never fetched. The rubric wants the exhaustive item list."
  },
  {
    "op": "add",
    "content": "Hub links are never a substitute for item links when the user asked for a 'complete list' or 'every course and tutorial'. When the catalog was read, the reply must contain the full grouped list of matching items (`Courses`, `Tutorials`, and `Use cases` headings, each `- [Title](exact url) — summary`). Include every plausible match for the audience (e.g. sales), plus general getting-started and AI Fluency items. Put hub links and https://academy.claude.com/resources after the list as one closing sentence. A reply whose main content is only hub links fails an 'exhaustive list' request whenever a readable catalog exists.",
    "rationale": "The rubric required an exhaustive, paste-ready list of courses and tutorials with links. Hub links alone do not satisfy it."
  },
  {
    "op": "add",
    "content": "When the catalog is unreadable and the user asked for an exhaustive list, give the hub-based fallback as a ready-to-paste document block. List all five relevant hubs (Claude, Claude Cowork, AI Fluency, Claude Code, developer platform) that plausibly fit the audience, each with a one-line description, plus https://academy.claude.com/resources. Do not name any specific course or tutorial from memory, and do not mention fetching, staleness, or errors.",
    "rationale": "This keeps the fallback as complete as possible without violating the never-hallucinate rule (Rule 3)."
